WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.630
Let's now design the algorithm for solving
the genome assembly problem.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.780
Let's have a string and its 3-mer
position,

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:15.690
which is a set of all 3-mers generated
from the string.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:21.240
If we represent each k-mer as a node, then

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.030
the genome is a path through all the
nodes.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:31.090
But can we construct this path without
knowing the

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:34.980
genome but only from its composition?

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.370
Well, yes we can!

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:45.130
We simply need to connect two k-mers if
they overlap by a (k-1)-mer.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:50.190
In this case, for example, we have TAA and
AAT here.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.295
We connect them by

00:00:51.295 --> 00:00:51.450
an edge
13
00:00:51,450 --&gt; 00:00:54,155
because they overlap by AA.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.200
Let's try to follow this idea.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:08.860
We connect all the nodes that overlap
by 2-mers. 

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.270
Is it all?

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.040
Can we construct the genome path already?

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.588
No, there are more connections.

00:01:15.588 --> 00:01:21.450
Because these k-mers also overlap by 2-mers 

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.280
Can we still find the genome path in this
graph?

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.310
I can!
How?

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.130
This is the way.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.700
Please note that I cheated a little bit
here because

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.290
these nodes are ordered according to their
positions in the genome.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:52.249
Well, in reality the position information
is not available.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:02:01.820
So if I slightly reorder the nodes in this
graph, then the task is more challenging.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.720
For example, in this case nodes are
arranged from left to right in

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.494
lexicographic order.
So the genome is actually a walk

00:02:12.494 --> 00:02:17.480
in this graph.
Yes, and this path

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:23.556
actually, what is the

00:02:23.556 --> 00:02:30.050
thing that we are trying to find?

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:36.550
We are trying to find the path that
traverses every node exactly once.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.570
And we call a path that visits each node
in

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:49.750
a graph exactly once, a Hamiltonian path
in the graph.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.520
And the Hamiltonian path problem is that
for a given graph, we need

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.160
to find a path that visits every node in
the graph exactly once.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.590
For example, let's look at this graph.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.000
This is a direct graph.
Is there a Hamiltonian

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.170
path in this graph?
Yes, there is.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.700
But it's a little bit tricky to find.
Here it is.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.929
Indeed this is actually the game invented
by William Hamilton, a great mathematician.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:29.815
But Hamiltonian path can also be

00:03:29.815 --> 00:03:36.375
applied for non directed graph as well,

00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:42.349
where there is no arrow on the edges.

